%I A399745 #7 Sep 28 2026 23:06:32
%S A399745 0,26,38,44,74,104,110,112,113,132,142,158
%N A399745 Numbers k such that the k-th Lucas number cannot be represented as a signed sum of entries from row k of Pascal's triangle, with each position used at most once.
%C A399745 This is the sequence of indices k for which A399744(k) = 0.
%C A399745 Other terms include 182, 202, 386, 832, 1672, 2032, 2272, 2273, and 31250.
%H A399745 Felix Huber, <a href="https://felixhuber-math.github.io/papers/digital-obstructions-lucas-pascal.pdf">Digital Obstructions to Signed Pascal Representations of Lucas Numbers</a>, preprint, 2026.
%F A399745 If p >= 11 is prime, u >= 1, q = 2*p^u - 1 is prime, and (5/q) = -1, then 2*p^u is a term; where (5/q) denotes the Legendre symbol.
%F A399745 If p > 23 is prime, p == 1 (mod 5), u >= 1, and p^u + 2 is prime, then p^u + 3 is a term.
%F A399745 If e >= 1 and 5^e + 1 = 2*p^u = q^v + 3, where p >= 11 and q > 19 are primes, u,v >= 1, and (5/q)^v = -1, then 5^e + 1 is a term.
%F A399745 If e >= 3 and both 2*5^e - 1 and 2*5^e - 3 are prime, then 2*5^e is a term.
%F A399745 If r >= 37 is prime, r == 2 (mod 5), and both 3*r - 4 and 3*r - 2 are prime, then 3*r + 1 is a term; if, in addition, 3*r + 2 is prime, then 3*r + 2 is also a term.
%e A399745 a(2) = 26, since A399744(26) = 0 and A399744(k) > 0 for 1 <= k < 26.
%e A399745 The consecutive terms 112 and 113 arise from r = 37 in the prime-pattern family above: 3*r - 4 = 107 and 3*r - 2 = 109 are prime, and 3*r + 2 = 113 is prime.
%p A399745 A399745 := proc(N)
%p A399745     local k;
%p A399745     [seq(`if`(A399744(k) = 0, k, NULL), k = 1 .. N)]
%p A399745 end proc:
%p A399745 A399745(44);
%Y A399745 Cf. A000032, A007318, A399743, A399744.
%K A399745 nonn,more,new
%O A399745 1,2
%A A399745 _Felix Huber_, Sep 16 2026
